          '**Burn Depth Classification**:\n- **Superficial (first-degree)**: Epidermis only (face — now healed). Painful, erythematous, no blistering. Heals in 5-7 days without scarring.\n- **Superficial partial-thickness (second-degree)**: Epidermis + superficial dermis. Moist, pink, blistering, blanches with pressure, very painful. Heals in 10-14 days. Low scar risk.\n- **Deep partial-thickness (second-degree)**: Epidermis + deep dermis (into reticular layer). Mottled pink/white, less blanching, reduced sensation. Heals in 3-6 weeks but with significant scarring. Often requires grafting.\n- **Full-thickness (third-degree)**: Entire dermis destroyed. Leathery, waxy, insensate. Cannot heal by re-epithelialization — requires STSG. **This patient has deep partial and full-thickness burns on extremities and chest.**\n\n**Contracture Development Timeline**:\n- **Acute phase (0-2 weeks)**: Edema and wound contraction begin. Anti-contracture positioning is critical.\n- **Proliferative phase (2 weeks - 6 months)**: **Highest risk for contracture**. Active wound contraction, myofibroblast activity, collagen deposition. This is the current phase for this patient.\n- **Remodeling/Maturation phase (6 months - 2 years)**: Collagen reorganization. Scars soften and flatten. Continued risk of contracture without ongoing management.\n\n**Key Contracture Risk Areas in This Patient**:\n- **Axillary contracture** (shoulder adduction/IR posture)\n- **Antecubital contracture** (elbow flexion)\n- **Wrist flexion contracture**\n- **MCP hyperextension contracture** (claw deformity) — MCPs should be positioned in 70-90 degrees of flexion\n- **First web space contracture** (thumb adduction)\n- **Neck flexion/anterior contracture**\n\n**Compartment Syndrome in Circumferential Burns** (acute phase concern):\n- **Signs**: Pain out of proportion, pain with passive stretch of distal muscles, tense/firm compartment, paresthesias, diminished pulses (late finding), compartment pressures >30 mmHg\n- **Management**: Emergent **escharotomy** (incision through burn eschar to release pressure) — does not require anesthesia in full-thickness burns (insensate)\n- In extremities, escharotomy is performed along medial and lateral aspects\n- **Fasciotomy** may be needed if pressure remains elevated after escharotomy',
